{
 "ep": 22,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Password Authentication and SCRAM",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Is your PostgreSQL password authentication secure?",
   "cap": "Is your *password authentication* secure?"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twenty two of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: how passwords are stored, and how to move to SCRAM.",
   "cap": "Episode 22: *how passwords are stored,* and *SCRAM.*",
   "cue": "SCRAM"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_hba.conf offers three password methods.",
   "cap": "*Three* password methods.",
   "cue": "methods"
  },
  {
   "say": "password sends it in clear text. Avoid it unless the connection uses SSL.",
   "cap": "*password* = clear text. Avoid it without *SSL.*",
   "cue": "SSL"
  },
  {
   "say": "md5 stops sniffing, but gives no protection if the stored hash is stolen.",
   "cap": "*md5:* weak if the *hash is stolen.*",
   "cue": "stolen"
  },
  {
   "say": "scram-sha-256 stores only a salted hash. The docs call it the most secure.",
   "cap": "*scram-sha-256:* the *most secure.*",
   "cue": "secure"
  },
  {
   "say": "But older client libraries may not support it.",
   "cap": "*Older client libraries* may lack SCRAM.",
   "cue": "libraries"
  },
  {
   "say": "The stored format comes from password_encryption, when the password is set.",
   "cap": "Format = *password_encryption* at set time.",
   "cue": "set"
  },
  {
   "say": "The default is scram-sha-256. The other value is md5.",
   "cap": "Default: *scram-sha-256.* Other: *md5.*",
   "cue": "md5"
  },
  {
   "say": "We made app_user with the default, and old_app with md5, then read pg_authid.",
   "cap": "Two users. Read *pg_authid.*",
   "cue": "pg_authid"
  },
  {
   "say": "Same server, two formats. Hashes trimmed.",
   "cap": "*SCRAM-SHA-256$* vs *md5.*",
   "cue": "two"
  },
  {
   "say": "Inside the SCRAM secret, four thousand ninety six is the scram_iterations default.",
   "cap": "*4096* = default *scram_iterations.*",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now the trap. A scram-sha-256 line in pg_hba.conf.",
   "cap": "The trap: a *scram-sha-256* line.",
   "cue": "line"
  },
  {
   "say": "old_app typed the right password, and was refused.",
   "cap": "Right password. *Refused.*",
   "cue": "refused"
  },
  {
   "say": "Only the server log said why: no valid SCRAM secret.",
   "cap": "Log: *no valid SCRAM secret.*",
   "cue": "SCRAM"
  },
  {
   "say": "The reverse works: an md5 line accepts a SCRAM password, and uses SCRAM.",
   "cap": "*md5* line + SCRAM password = *works.*",
   "cue": "uses"
  },
  {
   "say": "Migration, per the docs: check client libraries, and set password_encryption to scram-sha-256.",
   "cap": "Check clients. Set *password_encryption.*",
   "cue": "password_encryption"
  },
  {
   "say": "Have every user set a new password. Only then, change pg_hba.conf.",
   "cap": "*New passwords* first. *Then* pg_hba.conf.",
   "cue": "then"
  },
  {
   "say": "Find who is left: rolpassword like md5 percent.",
   "cap": "Find leftovers: *LIKE 'md5%'.*",
   "cue": "percent"
  },
  {
   "say": "Reset each with psql backslash password. It keeps the password out of history and logs.",
   "cap": "*\\password:* out of *history and logs.*",
   "cue": "history"
  },
  {
   "say": "Zero rows left, and old_app now logs in.",
   "cap": "*0 rows.* old_app *logs in.*",
   "cue": "logs"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: switch pg_hba.conf first, and md5 users are locked out.",
   "cap": "Switch first = *md5 users locked out.*",
   "cue": "locked"
  },
  {
   "say": "The current docs also say MD5 password support is deprecated.",
   "cap": "Current docs: MD5 passwords *deprecated.*",
   "cue": "deprecated"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: use scram-sha-256. Find md5 users, reset them, then switch pg_hba.conf.",
   "cap": "Recap: *find, reset, then switch.*",
   "cue": "switch"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: connection limits and max_connections. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *max_connections.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
